<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nicene Creed</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/325">325</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a> · Also known as Niceno-Constantinopolitan Creed, Credo</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Nicene Creed is the original statement of belief of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicene-christianity">Nicene Christianity</a>, adopted at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-council-of-nicaea">First Council of Nicaea</a> in 325 to resolve the Arian controversy over the divinity of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>. According to the traditional view, put forward by the Council of Chalcedon of 451, it was amended in 381 by the First Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>; the amended form is known as the Niceno-Constantinopolitan Creed. It is professed by the Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a>, Oriental Orthodox, Catholic, Lutheran, Anglican and many other churches, and is said or sung at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eucharist">Eucharist</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nicene-creed.jpg" alt="Nicene Creed" width="330" height="447" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nicaea_icon.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>A creed was called in Greek a symbolon, originally half of a broken object that, fitted to the other half, verified the bearer's identity; creeds were drawn up at times of doctrinal conflict to distinguish believers from heretics. The Nicene Creed was adopted to settle the Arian controversy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arius">Arius</a>, a clergyman of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, objected to the bishop Alexander's blurring of the distinction of nature between the Father and the Son. Emperor Constantine called the council at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicaea">Nicaea</a>, which opened on 19 June 325, because Arius' teachings threatened to destabilise the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>. F. J. A. Hort and Adolf von Harnack argued that the creed was the local creed of Caesarea, but more recent scholarship thinks the drafters began from a Syro-Palestinian creed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The creed of 325 affirms the Father as the one God and Jesus Christ as the Son of God, &quot;begotten of the essence of the Father&quot; and consubstantial with the Father, of the same substance. It mentions the Holy Spirit but not as God, and ends with anathemas against Arian propositions. The revised text, traditionally adopted at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 381, adds a section on the Holy Spirit &quot;who with the Father and the Son together is worshipped and glorified&quot;, and on the Church, baptism and the resurrection of the dead.</p><p>The origin of this text is disputed. The acts of the council of 381 were lost, and no extant document gives its text before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-chalcedon">Council of Chalcedon</a> in 451, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> greeted it with scepticism before it was produced from the archives of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. Many scholars hold that it was an older baptismal creed modified to resemble the Nicene, though most consider that it was introduced in 381.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Late in the sixth century some Latin-speaking churches added Filioque, &quot;and the Son&quot;, to the procession of the Holy Spirit; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> adopted it in its liturgy in 1014, and it became one of the leading causes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-west-schism">East-West Schism</a> of 1054. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Eastern Orthodox Church</a> recites the creed in the singular, &quot;I believe&quot;, and without the Filioque. The creed is professed by the Eastern Orthodox, Oriental Orthodox, Catholic, Lutheran, Anglican, Methodist and many other churches, and is said after the sermon at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eucharist">Eucharist</a> on Sundays and major feasts. Non-Trinitarian groups such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jehovahs-witnesses">Jehovah's Witnesses</a> reject its doctrine.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nicene Creed” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The creeds, the Apostles’ Creed and the <b>Nicene Creed</b> above all, summarise the faith shared by most Christians.</blockquote><a href="/religion/christianity">Christianity · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li><li><blockquote>Mormonism does not hold the <b>Nicene Creed</b>'s doctrine that the three are of one substance, and teaches that God organised the world from existing matter and did not create it from nothing.</blockquote><a href="/religion/mormonism">Mormonism · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li><li><blockquote>The theory rests on claims that are unproven, and the hymn draws almost all of its content from the Bible and the <b>Nicene Creed</b>; the Irish musicologist William H. Grattan Flood concluded that its words and music came from a Catholic source for Catholic worship.</blockquote><a href="/songs/o-come-all-ye-faithful">O Come, All Ye Faithful</a></li><li><blockquote>It had condemned Arianism, adopted the <b>Nicene Creed</b> and set rules for dating Easter, shaping Christian doctrine for centuries.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-25">25 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The First Council of Nicaea, convened by the emperor Constantine I, adopted the original <b>Nicene Creed</b>, whose text ends with anathemas against Arian propositions.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-19">19 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>It confirmed the original <b>Nicene Creed</b> and condemned Nestorius, who preferred to call the Virgin Mary Christotokos over Theotokos.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-22">22 June in history</a></li></ul></section>
